I actually don't believe that this was an oversight by the developers. Although they rewrote all this particular code in MZ, they still did it essentially the same way (where it calls the render function outside of the time-corrected part of the loop), so I am leaning toward the assumption that it's intentional.
Graphics stuff isn't really my forte, but if I were to venture a guess as to why they would choose to do this, I'd say that it could be because they wanted to reduce the chances of screen tearing. Although screen tearing most commonly happens when the game renders faster than your monitor's refresh rate, it can also still happen (albeit less noticeably) when the reverse is true, because the graphics card might try to push a new frame to the monitor during the middle of a refresh cycle.
Even if it means re-rendering unchanged frames, synchronizing the game's frame rate with the monitor's refresh rate will help align frame buffer updates with the monitor's refresh intervals, which should help prevent that tearing.
I don't think that this is particularly unusual. Many games that I've played seem to have the game logic and input processing decoupled from the rendering.
